1
00:00:11,985 --> 00:00:25,502
[SPEAKER_00]: So, something that I think a lot of people who are coming to try to see and understand the dhamma, there are a few avenues that we can take towards the brahma-viharas.

2
00:00:26,713 --> 00:00:32,017
[SPEAKER_00]: There's kind of the common view that this is a simple practice that anybody can just do.

3
00:00:32,337 --> 00:00:38,101
[SPEAKER_00]: You can just tell somebody, take somebody off the street and say, oh, go sit in your room.

4
00:00:38,762 --> 00:00:44,866
[SPEAKER_00]: Think of like a cute kitty cat and kind of that energy that you feel, that like that love you feel.

5
00:00:45,526 --> 00:00:50,430
[SPEAKER_00]: If you can direct that to everybody, even your enemies, then you're cultivating the brahma viharas.

6
00:00:52,916 --> 00:00:53,816
[SPEAKER_00]: And there's also...

7
00:00:54,837 --> 00:00:56,117
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can kind of take that avenue.

8
00:00:56,137 --> 00:00:57,678
[SPEAKER_00]: If this is easy, anybody can do it.

9
00:00:58,218 --> 00:01:02,200
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, you can kind of say these are really esoteric mystical states.

10
00:01:04,281 --> 00:01:12,064
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm not going to really worry about this at all until, you know, later, once I kind of have gotten all this other stuff out of the way.

11
00:01:13,384 --> 00:01:15,745
[SPEAKER_00]: And almost putting it on a pedestal that's too high.

12
00:01:16,806 --> 00:01:22,348
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I guess my question is, what is a practical way of approaching

13
00:01:23,125 --> 00:01:35,791
[SPEAKER_00]: The topic of the Brahma-Viharas that is not putting it on a pedestal that's too high or kind of, hopefully, kind of pulling it down to say, oh, well, yeah, this is easy.

14
00:01:36,151 --> 00:01:44,695
[SPEAKER_00]: I can do this easily.

15
00:01:44,715 --> 00:01:46,176
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, the the first thing that

16
00:01:48,670 --> 00:02:00,974
[SPEAKER_03]: Essentially, any discussion should begin with the point that you don't get the right view, you don't achieve stream-entry by doing some practice that then gives you a result.

17
00:02:01,994 --> 00:02:07,376
[SPEAKER_03]: You reach stream-entry by understanding how to do any practice that the Buddha taught.

18
00:02:08,156 --> 00:02:13,377
[SPEAKER_03]: Because, as he said, all wholesome things converge in the Four Noble Truths.

19
00:02:14,838 --> 00:02:15,738
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you need to have

20
00:02:16,377 --> 00:02:22,341
[SPEAKER_03]: That sense of what the problem is, in order to develop these practices that are treatments for the problem.

21
00:02:23,822 --> 00:02:34,109
[SPEAKER_03]: And so, that would undercut that sense of, like, okay, I'll just do this, and then I'll reach that result, because you would be assuming that you're already doing it properly, and that's really the problem.

22
00:02:34,129 --> 00:02:38,712
[SPEAKER_03]: Whereas, it should be more of a sense of whether it's Pramavihāras or anything else.

23
00:02:40,137 --> 00:02:43,020
[SPEAKER_03]: Understanding how to do this is stream-entry.

24
00:02:43,220 --> 00:02:53,609
[SPEAKER_03]: Understanding the path to liberation from suffering, understanding right mindfulness, understanding right composure, understanding right effort, understanding wholesome and unwholesome.

25
00:02:54,550 --> 00:02:56,011
[SPEAKER_03]: These things are stream-entry.

26
00:02:57,132 --> 00:02:58,053
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that should be the goal.

27
00:02:58,253 --> 00:03:00,275
[SPEAKER_03]: Now, eh...

28
00:03:03,443 --> 00:03:12,428
[SPEAKER_03]: Why, then, would... what would be the problem with what you said would be the normal way of approaching that?

29
00:03:12,548 --> 00:03:19,532
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, just thinking of it as a fabrication of beautiful, pleasant thoughts.

30
00:03:22,373 --> 00:03:23,874
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, there's no discernment.

31
00:03:23,974 --> 00:03:29,197
[SPEAKER_00]: You just kind of start with the assumption, I already know what this is, so...

32
00:03:29,677 --> 00:03:30,638
[SPEAKER_00]: But no discernment of what?

33
00:03:31,862 --> 00:03:36,264
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, no kind of clarification of your own views.

34
00:03:36,284 --> 00:03:42,406
[SPEAKER_00]: No clarification of the, like, kind of what you're bringing to the practice, so to speak.

35
00:03:42,606 --> 00:03:54,110
[SPEAKER_00]: And, I mean, if you have faith in what the Buddha says, then what he says is the problem has to do with these things that you're bringing.

36
00:03:55,210 --> 00:03:56,191
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

37
00:03:57,272 --> 00:04:05,538
[SPEAKER_03]: So, in that case, what would be the assumption that you would be bringing to the table when you're doing these practices?

38
00:04:11,283 --> 00:04:19,669
[SPEAKER_00]: I guess I would think that there could be a few different assumptions, or maybe it's different ways of kind of explaining the same thing, but

39
00:04:21,083 --> 00:04:26,956
[SPEAKER_00]: What comes to mind for me is that you think you know what needs to be kind of developed or abandoned.

40
00:04:27,778 --> 00:04:29,261
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you think that...

41
00:04:30,461 --> 00:04:39,344
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, the point of metta, for example, is I need to stop being bothered by this, you know, this mouse that's squeaking.

42
00:04:41,104 --> 00:04:43,605
[SPEAKER_00]: It needs to stop being unpleasant, for example.

43
00:04:43,685 --> 00:04:55,949
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I'm going to, like, cultivate that, like, when I hear this mouse squeaking or when somebody, like, breaks down my door and steals all my things, I'll just, like, be like, wow, I love you, man.

44
00:04:56,469 --> 00:04:57,630
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what would be

45
00:04:59,343 --> 00:05:04,186
[SPEAKER_03]: How would you phrase that in terms of... like, why is that not in line with the path, let's say?

46
00:05:04,466 --> 00:05:10,850
[SPEAKER_03]: Because you can say that, but it might not be necessarily obvious to people why that's a problem.

47
00:05:11,230 --> 00:05:16,173
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it means you assume feeling is the problem.

48
00:05:19,915 --> 00:05:22,117
[SPEAKER_00]: To give kind of a robotic answer.

49
00:05:23,678 --> 00:05:24,118
[SPEAKER_03]: Which means?

50
00:05:26,003 --> 00:05:27,904
[SPEAKER_00]: That you're not seeing craving is the problem.

51
00:05:28,224 --> 00:05:33,606
[SPEAKER_03]: So, as I just mentioned before, the Four Noble Truths are where all wholesome things converge.

52
00:05:35,267 --> 00:05:46,592
[SPEAKER_00]: This is kind of why you can say the right things, and it doesn't necessarily mean that you've... kind of... it doesn't mean that you've understood it in that kind of direct sense.

53
00:05:46,672 --> 00:05:46,832
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

54
00:05:46,852 --> 00:05:54,675
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, I can sit here and say, well, that way that, you know, that means you assume that craving... that feeling is the problem, which means you're overlooking craving, but...

55
00:05:55,428 --> 00:06:03,709
[SPEAKER_00]: That doesn't mean that when it comes down to it, I'm necessarily going to, like, align that idea with what's happening.

56
00:06:03,869 --> 00:06:04,110
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

57
00:06:04,950 --> 00:06:05,850
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

58
00:06:05,990 --> 00:06:07,290
[SPEAKER_04]: So, how can you prepare?

59
00:06:07,830 --> 00:06:18,572
[SPEAKER_04]: So, what is the best way to prepare yourself for situations when you do find intense anger arising due to circumstances?

60
00:06:18,652 --> 00:06:23,373
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, do you need to wait for it to happen and then recollect these instructions?

61
00:06:24,113 --> 00:06:24,213
[SPEAKER_04]: Or,

62
00:06:27,236 --> 00:06:33,518
[SPEAKER_00]: Or you can... well, the problem with waiting is you kind of assume it doesn't apply to you right now.

63
00:06:34,078 --> 00:06:34,658
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, so...

64
00:06:35,218 --> 00:06:52,282
[SPEAKER_04]: So, even right now, when the mind is not angry, not overcome by ill will, and no external circumstances are bothering it particularly, what would the right practice of metta, in this case, then, consist of?

65
00:06:55,615 --> 00:07:00,918
[SPEAKER_04]: Just to tell us the idea that you can only practice this when actually somebody is being angry with you.

66
00:07:01,058 --> 00:07:02,159
[SPEAKER_04]: Oh, and then...

67
00:07:02,179 --> 00:07:02,399
[SPEAKER_04]: Right?

68
00:07:02,439 --> 00:07:04,440
[SPEAKER_04]: Because that can be a view that people have.

69
00:07:04,460 --> 00:07:08,823
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, I need to wait to be in that situation and then I can apply this instruction that I have received.

70
00:07:08,863 --> 00:07:11,165
[SPEAKER_04]: You just put it on the shelf somewhere and you just take it and...

71
00:07:11,565 --> 00:07:14,367
[SPEAKER_04]: But that's not mind development then.

72
00:07:18,689 --> 00:07:20,250
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you're on your own, in your kuti.

73
00:07:20,370 --> 00:07:21,631
[SPEAKER_04]: There's not even anyone there.

74
00:07:23,212 --> 00:07:24,393
[SPEAKER_04]: Can you then still practice metta?

75
00:07:31,123 --> 00:07:31,703
[SPEAKER_00]: I guess there...

76
00:07:34,844 --> 00:07:52,807
[SPEAKER_00]: I guess there would have to be, kind of, on some level, something that is... Like, even if you are content, and you say, well, there's nothing that I'm kind of discontented about to practice metta with.

77
00:07:56,767 --> 00:08:00,288
[SPEAKER_00]: So long as you're not free from suffering, there is going to be, on some level,

78
00:08:02,647 --> 00:08:05,208
[SPEAKER_00]: Something that can be taken as discontent.

79
00:08:05,628 --> 00:08:21,276
[SPEAKER_00]: And this might be wrong, I'm not entirely sure, but you, like, in that case, kind of, you're... well, it might be the case that, like, you know, I'm wanting to practice metta, but there's nothing for me to, like, practice it with regard to.

80
00:08:21,296 --> 00:08:29,860
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, well, that kind of frustration, which is a bit more subtle than, like, we're used to kind of being angry about, like,

81
00:08:35,156 --> 00:08:38,677
[SPEAKER_00]: But can you just start with that subtle level?

82
00:08:39,037 --> 00:08:39,277
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

83
00:08:39,657 --> 00:08:49,161
[SPEAKER_00]: Not unless you kind of have a habit in place of doing this with regard to the more coarse things.

84
00:08:49,321 --> 00:08:53,962
[SPEAKER_03]: So what would be the more coarse things that you do practice mantra with, even though they're not necessarily other people?

85
00:08:55,083 --> 00:08:55,483
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I guess

86
00:08:57,040 --> 00:09:03,966
[SPEAKER_00]: That kind of leads me to say that practice, keeping the precepts, could be, like, or that is, developing that.

87
00:09:04,627 --> 00:09:15,677
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, well, keeping the precepts with the, at the same time, understanding that, like you said, you're, you don't want to just,

88
00:09:17,857 --> 00:09:19,760
[SPEAKER_03]: You do it with a mindset of discontent.

89
00:09:19,841 --> 00:09:24,689
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you want to reach a point where keeping the precepts... while keeping the precepts, your mind is content.

90
00:09:25,510 --> 00:09:30,980
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, that's another confusion that people will think it's like only about people.

91
00:09:31,745 --> 00:09:37,448
[SPEAKER_03]: But really, the principle of ill-will is just in regard to how you feel.

92
00:09:37,648 --> 00:09:39,209
[SPEAKER_03]: It's just a resistance to how you feel.

93
00:09:39,869 --> 00:09:44,591
[SPEAKER_03]: So, other people and situations might act as a trigger for that.

94
00:09:45,172 --> 00:09:48,173
[SPEAKER_03]: But that tendency is there in relation to anything that you feel.

95
00:09:48,873 --> 00:09:53,736
[SPEAKER_03]: And so, that's why, if you train yourself, for example, to put up with the discomfort of

96
00:09:54,516 --> 00:09:55,557
[SPEAKER_03]: Which is where you have to start.

97
00:09:56,877 --> 00:10:02,520
[SPEAKER_03]: Always, the Buddha said, you train yourself in these things first, and then you begin these mental cultivation practices.

98
00:10:03,060 --> 00:10:12,885
[SPEAKER_03]: With virtue, like, there isn't any fundamental difference between that displeasure that you experience when you stop giving in to desires,

99
00:10:13,785 --> 00:10:18,348
[SPEAKER_03]: And the displeasure that you feel when somebody upsets you, or something like that.

100
00:10:18,468 --> 00:10:19,128
[SPEAKER_03]: It's exactly that.

101
00:10:19,308 --> 00:10:22,790
[SPEAKER_03]: If you train yourself in regard to one, you'll be training yourself in regard to the other.

102
00:10:22,850 --> 00:10:40,121
[SPEAKER_03]: So that's why, eh... if somebody is... you can actually tell, like, just practice those eight precepts or something for six months, and you'll see, eh... even if you haven't made that great effort to to develop wisdom, you will see that you'll be less... more difficult to make you angry.

103
00:10:42,502 --> 00:11:08,535
[SPEAKER_01]: Another issue with this view of just fabricating these corny thoughts as a practice is that you don't realize, actually, the reason why you have ill-will... Would you be able to have ill-will or get angry or get annoyed if you don't have attachments?

104
00:11:12,624 --> 00:11:26,607
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why, like, you can be doing all of this, spending eight hours a day practicing metta, but throughout your day, you keep shoving fuel into that fire of desire.

105
00:11:28,068 --> 00:11:30,771
[SPEAKER_01]: The liability to the will is being fed.

106
00:11:31,372 --> 00:11:39,541
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, like, okay, I have all these possessions, I have all these, you know, friends, family, pleasures that I depend on.

107
00:11:40,282 --> 00:11:47,470
[SPEAKER_01]: And as soon as anybody or anything brings those things under threat,

108
00:11:48,378 --> 00:11:50,500
[SPEAKER_01]: I will be liable immediately.

109
00:11:50,680 --> 00:11:51,781
[SPEAKER_01]: The ill will will be there.

110
00:11:52,642 --> 00:11:55,404
[SPEAKER_01]: And that is actually the root of ill will.

111
00:11:55,484 --> 00:11:58,427
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I mean, they're like two sides of the same coin.

112
00:11:58,907 --> 00:12:07,855
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's why even if you never make effort to practice metta, but you free yourself from any dependence on any pleasure, any attachment, you will have a mind of metta.

113
00:12:08,447 --> 00:12:12,390
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, because there would be less or nothing at all for you to defend.

114
00:12:12,530 --> 00:12:27,862
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, and then you might not necessarily see, because then, if you confuse that metta with like the specific positive thinking or whatever, then you might not see that actually that's a by-product of the mind of metta.

115
00:12:27,942 --> 00:12:35,067
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not even... it's not even necessarily what defines it.

116
00:12:36,243 --> 00:12:39,605
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, something doesn't need to be smoking in order to be hot.

117
00:12:41,947 --> 00:12:43,448
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's more of a side effect.

118
00:12:44,008 --> 00:12:52,593
[SPEAKER_01]: And so, it's eh... Then, you might see somebody who is, for example, an arahant, like free from suffering, like the Buddha himself, actually.

119
00:12:53,514 --> 00:12:56,676
[SPEAKER_01]: And, eh... you see in the suttas, like, he never smiles.

120
00:12:58,490 --> 00:13:12,495
[SPEAKER_01]: And that would be something that, you know, people would confuse metta with that attitude of being super, kind of externally, with that overly friendly behavior.

121
00:13:13,556 --> 00:13:15,156
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's not what it is.

122
00:13:15,376 --> 00:13:23,999
[SPEAKER_01]: Because he himself, who he said, he has the highest metta, the highest compassion, the highest equanimity of all beings.

123
00:13:24,680 --> 00:13:25,040
[SPEAKER_01]: And yet,

124
00:13:25,760 --> 00:13:31,602
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, you even read how he would sometimes rebuke and admonish monks and things like that.

125
00:13:33,523 --> 00:13:40,885
[SPEAKER_01]: And that shows that it's actually a byproduct of having cultivated a mind of metta.

126
00:13:41,506 --> 00:13:44,987
[SPEAKER_01]: So, all those thoughts that you see, for example, in the Metta Sutta,

127
00:13:45,908 --> 00:13:47,931
[SPEAKER_03]: May all beings be happy.

128
00:13:48,011 --> 00:13:49,614
[SPEAKER_03]: May they be free from illness.

129
00:13:49,954 --> 00:13:56,926
[SPEAKER_03]: May they be free from enmity and not have conflict with each other and whatnot.

130
00:13:57,762 --> 00:14:05,287
[SPEAKER_01]: Those thoughts would arise by themselves for somebody who has freed themselves from that liability to their will.

131
00:14:06,188 --> 00:14:09,150
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in other words, if you heat up the thing, it's going to start smoking.

132
00:14:09,870 --> 00:14:12,752
[SPEAKER_01]: But you don't, like, try to create the smoke directly.

133
00:14:12,832 --> 00:14:19,977
[SPEAKER_01]: Or another example would be, you know, how some people, like that, there's this idea that you fake it till you make it.

134
00:14:21,148 --> 00:14:38,763
[SPEAKER_01]: So, for example, you might see somebody who has a very confident demeanor, like a millionaire or something, who goes around, you know, with a very proud and confident expression, and just try to copy his mannerisms, without actually having all the things that give him that confidence.

135
00:14:39,443 --> 00:14:42,184
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, that's going to be like a facade.

136
00:14:42,224 --> 00:14:45,145
[SPEAKER_01]: It's going to crack easily.

137
00:14:45,285 --> 00:14:45,746
[SPEAKER_01]: It's hollow.

138
00:14:46,446 --> 00:14:53,429
[SPEAKER_01]: Whereas, if you actually develop yourself to gain those things that brought him that confidence, you will have it truly.

139
00:14:54,169 --> 00:14:56,570
[SPEAKER_01]: And you will not need to even think about faking it.

140
00:14:56,710 --> 00:15:01,392
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, sometimes, like, you wouldn't even think about how I move my hands or what I do.

141
00:15:01,452 --> 00:15:04,853
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just... it would be a natural by-product.

142
00:15:05,145 --> 00:15:09,346
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, and what is it, according to the dhamma, that brings confidence?

143
00:15:11,067 --> 00:15:11,687
[SPEAKER_00]: The right view.

144
00:15:12,847 --> 00:15:20,269
[SPEAKER_00]: Knowing what... kind of knowing what... to do, essentially.

145
00:15:20,809 --> 00:15:28,911
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, nobody's ever so confident, but also there's another factor, which is... which is described as that which... which makes people confident even before the right view.

146
00:15:32,172 --> 00:15:32,532
[SPEAKER_02]: Honesty?

147
00:15:33,313 --> 00:15:33,913
[SPEAKER_02]: Authenticity?

148
00:15:36,421 --> 00:15:37,006
[SPEAKER_04]: mā spasivakti.

149
00:15:43,198 --> 00:15:48,382
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, it says virtue is that which brings confidence.

150
00:15:48,622 --> 00:16:02,651
[SPEAKER_04]: So, when somebody is virtuous and then they make an offering, even to great beings, they will do so respectfully, confidently, because they have already, to some degree, that sense of blamelessness.

151
00:16:02,691 --> 00:16:07,495
[SPEAKER_04]: They are not divided against themselves, because they have cleared all that up on account of their virtue.

152
00:16:07,635 --> 00:16:09,836
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, and that's how you would actually get to it.

153
00:16:09,896 --> 00:16:11,357
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, by really

154
00:16:12,178 --> 00:16:27,978
[SPEAKER_01]: So to speak, getting your hands dirty and not acting out of anger in those situations that incite anger, whether through body, speech, or even eventually mind, that's how you would reach that confidence that, no, like, it's not that...

155
00:16:29,191 --> 00:16:36,675
[SPEAKER_01]: The ill will arises because of this sort of quick moment that I didn't catch, and I didn't start the practice soon enough, and I got out of hand.

156
00:16:37,415 --> 00:16:41,197
[SPEAKER_03]: You will realize that that's on account of your own choices, this was happening.

157
00:16:41,697 --> 00:16:46,039
[SPEAKER_03]: And that builds confidence, because now you know that that will not come back by itself.

158
00:16:46,539 --> 00:16:47,119
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

159
00:16:47,179 --> 00:16:49,140
[SPEAKER_03]: It's like you open the door and it comes in.

160
00:16:49,841 --> 00:16:59,446
[SPEAKER_03]: And you were doubtful and not confident before, because you didn't see that gateway where, you know, the ill will, you were allowing it in.

161
00:17:00,186 --> 00:17:06,470
[SPEAKER_01]: And so, that's why the whole point is, you need to then

162
00:17:07,656 --> 00:17:16,742
[SPEAKER_01]: Not see this pleasure that comes up when people annoy you, when people do things that you don't like.

163
00:17:18,343 --> 00:17:25,127
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to see that as what your practice happens in relation to.

164
00:17:26,428 --> 00:17:28,089
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, this pleasure is not the problem.

165
00:17:28,909 --> 00:17:30,990
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the whole point of the Four Noble Truths.

166
00:17:31,251 --> 00:17:32,451
[SPEAKER_01]: Feeling is not the issue.

167
00:17:32,711 --> 00:17:33,752
[SPEAKER_01]: Craving is the issue.

168
00:17:33,852 --> 00:17:36,534
[SPEAKER_01]: And craving is most acutely reflected by your actions.

169
00:17:37,529 --> 00:17:42,993
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you start from... you will not be able to purify your mental actions until you purify your bodily actions.

170
00:17:43,934 --> 00:17:45,315
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's how you would see that principle.

171
00:17:45,355 --> 00:17:57,443
