00:37:43,622 --> 00:37:47,525
[SPEAKER_01]: My liability to be angry with the person who does something to them.

369
00:37:47,625 --> 00:37:50,126
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, because you might have inadvertently been building up attachment.

370
00:37:50,186 --> 00:37:50,707
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

371
00:37:50,747 --> 00:38:02,434
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're building up selectiveness, and that's quite the opposite of the mind of metta, which is, as the description says, boundless, unlimited, and it's not fixed upon anything.

372
00:38:02,494 --> 00:38:02,674
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

373
00:38:03,375 --> 00:38:12,260
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it's... that's why it's closer, actually, to equanimity, which is why it would naturally culminate in equanimity, which is the fourth brahmavihāra, which is the highest.

374
00:38:12,461 --> 00:38:12,681
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

375
00:38:13,899 --> 00:38:17,716
[SPEAKER_01]: Whereas, I think most people have that background assumption that that one's kind of like...

376
00:38:19,117 --> 00:38:23,378
[SPEAKER_01]: Not as good, because it's kind of cold and harsh to be a quantumist.

377
00:38:24,158 --> 00:38:25,859
[SPEAKER_01]: But no, the Buddha is actually saying this is the best one.

378
00:38:26,179 --> 00:38:26,979
[SPEAKER_01]: This is the best of all.

379
00:38:27,759 --> 00:38:36,382
[SPEAKER_01]: So, metta would naturally culminate in that, because it's already closer to that, than your normal state of, like, random person just having nice thoughts.

380
00:38:37,982 --> 00:38:46,705
[SPEAKER_03]: It would take you beyond sensuality as well, because it's, as I said before, it's that same attitude of resisting this pleasure,

381
00:38:47,552 --> 00:39:01,896
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, that same displeasure that you get, for example, when you don't give your senses what they want, that is the same annoyance and discomfort in its nature that you experience when people do bad things to you.

382
00:39:01,976 --> 00:39:05,017
[SPEAKER_03]: So, if you overcome one liability, that aversion,

383
00:39:07,363 --> 00:39:10,224
[SPEAKER_03]: Towards your own feelings, you will overcome sensuality as well.

384
00:39:10,644 --> 00:39:11,604
[SPEAKER_03]: And the opposite applies.

385
00:39:12,004 --> 00:39:18,687
[SPEAKER_03]: If you have no attachments, if you have no desires, if you have no pleasures that you depend on, you cannot hate anyone.

386
00:39:18,887 --> 00:39:22,988
[SPEAKER_03]: Because there's nothing for you... there's nothing that you own that could be compromised.

387
00:39:23,525 --> 00:39:32,911
[SPEAKER_04]: I think that even this one sutta in the Majjhima, where it says that sensuality is really the origin of all hatred and ill will.

388
00:39:33,271 --> 00:39:38,654
[SPEAKER_04]: And then it goes step by step, and then it says all the way to the point of taking up sticks and fighting.

389
00:39:38,934 --> 00:39:41,195
[SPEAKER_04]: So, in our times, that's war.

390
00:39:41,456 --> 00:39:45,798
[SPEAKER_04]: And so, that's fascinating.

391
00:39:46,719 --> 00:39:50,321
[SPEAKER_04]: So, if you, even from a social perspective, if you don't want to have war,

392
00:39:51,202 --> 00:39:55,535
